abstract: >-
  Does pairing semantically dissimilar solutions for reciprocal peer
  review improve test-time reasoning of homogeneous LLM agents? In a
  controlled study (MMLU-Pro, 3 seeds, n=1500; SuperGPQA, 4 seeds,
  n=2000; one frozen model, shared cached initial answers across all
  pairing conditions), farthest-pair reciprocal review (FPRR) did not
  outperform random pairing, nearest pairing, maximum-weight matching,
  majority voting, or compute-matched self-review. Mechanistic analysis
  shows semantic distance monotonically increases error detection but
  not end-to-end correction: false opposition and destabilization of
  valid alternatives rise together with useful disagreement, making
  peer review active but net-zero. An exploratory ablation suggests
  NLI-contradiction distance is a more informative routing signal than
  cosine distance.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - multi-agent systems
  - test-time scaling
  - peer review
  - semantic diversity
  - negative results
